VI  - 336
DP  - 2026 May 21
TI  - Interpreting ECG Images with Multimodal Large Language Models.
PG  - 959-963
LID - 10.3233/SHTI260321 [doi]
AB  - Cardiac diseases are one of the leading causes of death worldwide. 
      Electrocardiography (ECG) is one of the major diagnostic methods to detect 
      cardiac diseases, including historical and acute myocardial infarction and 
      arrhythmias. Recent advancements in AI, especially the introduction of multimodal 
      large language models capable of understanding images, may provide a high level 
      of accuracy in interpreting ECG images for possible cardiac diseases. We 
      investigated Google Gemini 2.5 Pro and OpenAI GPT-4o for their abilities in 
      recognizing the cardiac conditions on a corpus of 928 annotated ECG images and 
      experimented on 10-shot and 5-shot classification methods, both with labelled 
      examples to the models. In the 5-shot approach, examples fed to the LLMs agree 
      with the annotation from a medical resident, and for 10-shot method, examples 
      were randomly chosen from the train set. Our results show that few-shot 
      classification methods significantly improved the accuracy with Gemini 2.5 Pro 
      achieving 0.635 (10-shot) and 0.543 (5-shot) in accuracies, and GPT-4o reaching 
      0.819 (10-shot) and 0.829 (5-shot) accuracy, demonstrating their ability to 
      interpret ECG images. Multimodal large language models may provide a cost 
      effective and accurate way to interpret ECG images.
