SB  - IM
OAB - People with migraine have higher rates of cardiovascular disease, yet clinicians 
      lack simple tools to predict which patients face the greatest risk. We studied 
      artificial intelligence‐based electrocardiogram, a quick test that shows heart 
      rate and rhythm, from nearly 30,000 patients with migraine and found that 
      artificial intelligence‐predicted risk and accelerated biological aging both 
      strongly predicted future heart attacks, strokes, and blood clots. These tests 
      can potentially help doctors identify who among their patients with migraine is 
      more likely to develop serious vascular issues.
