DP  - 2025 Jul
TI  - Vision Transformer-Based Classification Model for Estimating Left Atrium 
      Low-Voltage Area Using ECG Images.
PG  - 1-4
LID - 10.1109/EMBC58623.2025.11253868 [doi]
AB  - BACKGROUND: The low voltage area (LVA) in the left atrium has been demonstrated 
      to be associated with cardiac conditions, including atrial fibrillation (AF). 
      Thus, it is thought to be a potential indicator of cardiac function. Since LVA 
      measurement currently requires invasive catheter-based procedures, there is a 
      need to develop simple and non-invasive evaluation methods that facilitate early 
      detection and risk stratification mainly in AF patients. METHODS: We developed a 
      machine learning model for estimating left atrial LVA using non-invasively 
      measured electrocardiogram (ECG) data. Preoperative 12-lead ECG images and 
      intraoperatively measured left atrial LVA values [cm(2)] were collected from 
      atrial fibrillation patients who underwent catheter ablation procedures at four 
      clinical centers in Japan. Based on the LVA values, the image data were 
      categorized into two groups: normal (LVA = 0 cm(2)) and abnormal (LVA > 0 cm(2)). 
      A binary classification model was constructed to estimate the presence of LVA 
      using the Vision Transformer (ViT) as a feature extractor. RESULTS: We collected 
      preoperative 12-lead ECG images from 1,574 patients. 962 image data were labeled 
      as normal and 457 as abnormal. The rest were unlabeled due to missing LVA data in 
      the clinical records. The best model achieved an accuracy of 0.71 on average. 
      CONCLUSION: This study demonstrated the potential of evaluating the left atrial 
      LVA non-invasively through lead II ECG image analysis based on the ViT model, 
      suggesting its application as a decision support system in clinical practice. 
      CLINICAL RELEVANCE: This proposed method is expected to expand access to LVA 
      evaluation by enabling LVA assessment without the need for catheterization, which 
      realized personalized treatment planning and enhancing long-term outcomes for AF 
      patients. In addition, image-based analysis is expected to enhance the model 
      adaptability across clinical centers with varying ECG systems.
