VI  - 2025
DP  - 2025 Jul
TI  - Inverse Filtering for ECG Signal Reconstruction-A Deep Learning Approach.
PG  - 1-6
LID - 10.1109/EMBC58623.2025.11254051 [doi]
AB  - Large-scale screening for cardiac arrhythmia, such as atrial fibrillation, using 
      wearable and hand-held devices gain importance in modern diagnostics. However, 
      due to the electrode characteristics, these devices often incorporate signal 
      conditioning that potentially distorts the typical ECG morphology, significantly 
      aggravating the subsequent diagnosis. To overcome this issue, we propose a deep 
      learning-based inverse filtering approach using a U-Net autoencoder to 
      reconstruct clean single-lead ECG signals. A synthetic dataset was generated 
      using the MIMIC-IV database and an object-oriented model simulating the device's 
      characteristics, producing paired noisy and clean ECGs for training the neural 
      network. Evaluation of synthetic data showed effective reconstruction 
      capabilities, preserving key ECG features with an MSE of 0.052 on the validation 
      set and 0.134 on the test set. Additionally, the performance was evaluated on 
      real-world data recorded from the MyDiagnostick single-lead ECG device and a 
      reference patient monitor, yielding an overall improvement in extracting the most 
      important temporal ECG features such as PQ interval, QRS duration and QTc 
      time.Clinical relevance- The proposed deep learning-based inverse filtering 
      approach enhances the diagnostic utility of single-lead ECG devices by mitigating 
      signal distortions introduced by dry electrodes and filtering artifacts. By 
      improving the extraction of important temporal ECG features, such as PQ interval, 
      QRS duration, and QTc time, this method can help to enhance clinical 
      decision-making, particularly in large-scale screening for atrial fibrillation 
      and other cardiac conditions.
