TI  - How to screen for atrial fibrillation on a community level: Recent advances.
PG  - 997-1004
LID - 10.33963/v.phj.107879 [doi]
AB  - Atrial fibrillation (AF) is a common arrhythmia associated with elevated stroke 
      risk, often undiagnosed due to asymptomatic presentation. Advances in technology 
      have enabled AF detection beyond conventional hospital-based tools, expanding 
      opportunities for community-level screening. This review summarizes current 
      international guideline recommendations and recent studies evaluating 
      community-based AF screening strategies. Observational studies across diverse 
      global settings have demonstrated the feasibility and yield of screening using 
      tools such as handheld electrocardiogram devices, blood pressure monitors, and 
      smartphone-based technologies, with number-needed-to-screen ranging widely based 
      on population risk profiles. Randomized controlled trials, including STROKESTOP 
      and eBRAVE-AF, suggest potential benefits in stroke reduction and anticoagulant 
      uptake, but definitive consensus is still lacking. The emergence of consumer-led 
      screening using wearable devices (e.g., smartwatches, wristbands) shows promise 
      but raises questions about diagnostic yield and treatment impact in low-risk 
      populations. Cost-effectiveness analyses support screening, especially in older 
      adults, but highlight the need for tailored implementation based on local 
      healthcare systems. Future directions include risk stratification using 
      biomarkers, polygenic risk scores, and artificial intelligence models applied to 
      electrocardiogram or health records to identify high-risk individuals. While 
      enthusiasm for AF screening is growing, critical evidence gaps remain - 
      particularly regarding clinical outcomes and optimal screening strategies.
