TI  - Emerging rapid detection methods for the monitoring of cardiovascular diseases: 
      Current trends and future perspectives.
PG  - 101663
LID - 10.1016/j.mtbio.2025.101663 [doi]
LID - 101663
AB  - Cardiovascular diseases (CVDs) persist as the foremost cause of global mortality, 
      necessitating advancements in diagnostic and monitoring technologies to enable 
      timely intervention and effective management. This review synthesizes recent 
      technological innovations in rapid testing for CVDs, emphasizing the development 
      and integration of wearable sensors, and point-of-care (POC) testing systems. 
      Wearable sensors, including advanced electrocardiogram (ECG) monitors, 
      photoplethysmography (PPG) devices, and continuous glucose monitors (CGMs), 
      leverage novel materials to facilitate continuous, real-time physiological 
      monitoring. POC testing systems, integrate microfluidics with CRISPR-based 
      diagnostic platforms and artificial intelligence (AI)-driven analytics to deliver 
      rapid, on-site biomarker detection and real-time data interpretation. Advanced 
      signal processing and machine learning algorithms, underpin these technologies by 
      enhancing data fidelity, extracting pertinent features, and enabling predictive 
      analytics for proactive cardiovascular risk management. Despite significant 
      progress, challenges such as signal integrity, power management, data security, 
      and regulatory compliance persist. Future directions highlight the potential of 
      personalized medicine and interdisciplinary collaborations to further refine and 
      implement these rapid testing innovations, ultimately advancing cardiovascular 
      healthcare outcomes.
