PMID- 40040120
OWN - NLM
STAT- MEDLINE
DCOM- 20250511
LR  - 20250511
IS  - 2694-0604 (Electronic)
IS  - 2375-7477 (Linking)
VI  - 2024
DP  - 2024 Jul
TI  - Causal Inference Based Hierarchical Regression Model for Cuffless Blood Pressure 
      Estimation.
PG  - 1-4
LID - 10.1109/EMBC53108.2024.10782557 [doi]
AB  - The key to cuffless blood pressure (BP) estimation lies in identifying the 
      efficient noninvasive features or signals that can indicate BP changes. Most 
      these features are identified based on their correlation with BP. However, the 
      correlation does not imply causation, as there might be confounders impacting the 
      relationship between the features and BP. In this study, we introduce causal 
      inference to identify the confounders that can possibly affect the accuracy of 
      the commonly studied pulse transit time (PTT) based cuffless BP estimation. We 
      further propose a hierarchical regression model to address the impact of the 
      confounder thereby improving the estimation performance. We first build the 
      causal graph of BP and common features extracted from electrocardiogram (ECG) and 
      photoplethysmogram (PPG) signals with the fast causal inference algorithm and 
      causal generative neural network. We found heart rate is the main confounder that 
      impacting the relationship between PTT and BP. To consider the effect of the 
      confounder, we then developed a hierarchical regression model for cuffless BP 
      estimation. We validated the proposed method on 17 subjects, and the results show 
      that the mean absolute difference (MAD) of the estimated systolic BP and 
      diastolic BP were 6.29 mmHg and 5.03 mmHg respectively, which significantly 
      outperformed the linear regression model without the hierarchical strategy.
