VI  - 2024
DP  - 2024 Jul
TI  - Estimating Blood Pressure Measurements through an Electro-vascular-gram: A pilot 
      study.
PG  - 1-4
LID - 10.1109/EMBC53108.2024.10781793 [doi]
AB  - The ability to accurately measure blood pressure (BP) is considered an important 
      clinical tool in improving the health of patients with cardiovascular disease. In 
      this study, we investigated the use of a cuff electrode as a potential means of 
      predicting arterial BP. Acute experiments were conducted in urethane anesthetized 
      male rats (n = 3), where electrical artifact signals - termed the 
      electro-vascular-gram (EVG) - were measured using a cuff electrode surgically 
      implanted around the right carotid artery. The left carotid artery was 
      catheterized to directly measure BP (ground truth). The EVG signal was processed 
      and trained using a convolutional neural network (CNN), which could predict 
      systolic (SYS) and diastolic (DIA) pressure values (with an error < 5mmHg) in 
      45-98% and 52-97% of the testing datasets, respectively. Analysis of this 
      preliminary data suggests that it is feasible to use the EVG signal to measure BP 
      in a continuous manner.
