TI  - Compression and Encryption of Heterogeneous Signals for Internet of Medical 
      Things.
PG  - 2524-2535
LID - 10.1109/JBHI.2023.3264997 [doi]
AB  - Psychophysiological computing can be utilized to analyze heterogeneous 
      physiological signals with psychological behaviors in the Internet of Medical 
      Things (IoMT). Since IoMT devices are generally limited by power, storage, and 
      computing resources, it's very challenging to process the physiological signal 
      securely and efficiently. In this work, we design a novel scheme named 
      Heterogeneous Compression and Encryption Neural Network (HCEN), which aims to 
      protect signal security and reduce the required resources in processing 
      heterogeneous physiological signals. The proposed HCEN is designed as an 
      integrated structure that introduces the adversarial properties of Generative 
      Adversarial Networks (GAN) and the feature extraction functionality of 
      Autoencoder (AE). Moreover, we conduct simulations to validate the performance of 
      HCEN using the MIMIC-III waveform dataset. Electrocardiogram (ECG) and 
      Photoplethysmography (PPG) signals are extracted in the simulation. The results 
      reveal that the proposed HCEN can effectively encrypt floating-point signals. 
      Meanwhile, the compression performance outperforms baseline compression methods.
